WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Ben automated his entire job.

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:04.460
By Monday, he was working twelve minutes a day.

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:07.400
By Friday, his boss wanted to know his secret.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:08.680
That was the problem.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.020
Three weeks earlier,

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:13.360
Ben's job had been moving information between rectangles.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:15.100
An email became a spreadsheet.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:17.220
The spreadsheet became a report.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.560
The report became a presentation explaining the spreadsheet.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:21.660
Every Thursday,

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:23.980
someone asked for the same information

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:25.640
in a slightly different rectangle.

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:29.720
Ben changed the font, moved a column, and called the file final.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:30.940
Then final two.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:32.380
Then actually final.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:33.260
By lunch,

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:35.840
he had created a small family of documents

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:37.840
that disagreed about what day it was.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.160
His work wasn't difficult every minute.

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:41.840
But it occupied every minute.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.020
A report took twenty minutes to prepare

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:45.620
and forty minutes to explain.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.000
Whenever Ben finished early, he kept a spreadsheet open.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.180
Apparently, an open spreadsheet looked like effort.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:54.040
A closed spreadsheet looked like

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:56.260
a dangerous amount of available capacity.

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:59.080
One evening, he tried a small experiment.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.700
Using company approved tools and dummy data,

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.600
he built a workflow to handle the repetitive steps.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:06.380
Sort the sample messages.

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:08.480
Extract the relevant information.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:09.740
Draft the report.

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:11.640
Then stop for a human check.

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:14.160
He wasn't trying to replace every decision.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.740
He wanted to stop spending his best hours

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.000
moving the same information between boxes.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.320
The first version put a sandwich under business expenses.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.120
The second invented a customer called Customer.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.500
The third produced a beautiful chart showing

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:30.100
that Tuesday had increased by twelve percent.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:31.900
Ben stared at it for a long time.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.140
The computer had made the report faster.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.020
It had also made nonsense look ready for a meeting.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:38.920
Those were two separate achievements.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:40.440
So he added checks.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.120
Missing information went into a separate tray.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.180
Strange totals triggered a review.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:47.380
Unusual requests stayed with him.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:49.700
Ben still needed to understand the numbers.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.200
The machine could move a mistake very quickly.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.940
That wasn't the same as making the mistake disappear.

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:57.120
After several evenings of testing,

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.560
the boring part finally became shorter.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:00.520
Much shorter.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:01.600
On Monday morning,

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.680
the usual reports were ready before his coffee cooled.

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:06.200
Ben checked the totals.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:06.780
Correct.

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:08.100
Checked the attachments.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:09.000
Correct.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:11.320
Checked whether he was allowed to feel happy.

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:12.100
Unclear.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.360
He waited for another task to arrive.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:15.420
Nothing happened.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:18.080
The tiny factory on his screen was quiet.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:21.120
For the first time in months, so was his head.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:22.660
He took a real lunch break.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:26.420
He called his mother without simultaneously formatting a table.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:28.220
He walked outside and discovered

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:30.340
that daylight was available on weekdays.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:31.160
At five,

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.480
he still had enough energy to cook something

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.180
that required more than adding water.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:37.540
He used some of the extra time to fix

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:39.480
an old problem nobody had scheduled.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.820
Two departments were counting the same customer differently.

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:45.740
Ben spoke to both of them, found the mismatch,

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:47.320
and corrected the instructions.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:49.780
There was no dramatic button to press.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.440
Just a conversation and a careful decision.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.480
It prevented tomorrow's mistake,

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:56.820
which unfortunately looked less impressive

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:58.400
than today's colorful dashboard.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:01.180
On Thursday, Mara stopped at his desk.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:03.820
She had noticed that his reports were arriving early

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:05.300
and contained fewer corrections.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:08.480
Ben considered explaining that he had discovered vitamins.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:10.800
Instead, he showed her the workflow.

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:14.580
Mara watched a pile of routine paperwork turn into a short review.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:15.680
She smiled.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:16.560
Ben smiled.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:20.180
Somewhere, a very small alarm went off in Ben's brain.

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:22.440
Mara said this could help the whole team.

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:25.820
Ben imagined teaching everyone, reducing the backlog,

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.260
and finally having fewer urgent Fridays.

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:30.260
Mara imagined the same things.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.780
Unfortunately, neither of them said what

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:34.400
should happen to the time afterward.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:36.880
That blank space in the conversation would

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.020
soon become the busiest place in the office.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:42.400
The next morning, Ben received three people's paperwork.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:43.540
Same desk.

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:44.640
Same salary.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:45.660
Bigger rectangle.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:47.500
Ben assumed it was temporary.

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:50.440
He adjusted the workflow and finished the new batch.

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:52.200
Then another department appeared.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:55.260
Apparently, the reward for finding a shorter queue

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:57.700
was being introduced to several longer queues.

00:03:57.940 --> 00:03:59.100
He made another improvement.

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:00.360
More work arrived.

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:02.660
His inbox had become a vending machine

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:05.060
that accepted efficiency and dispensed obligations.

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:08.720
Soon, Ben was supervising a tiny digital factory

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:12.140
while answering messages about why the factory wasn't faster.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:14.860
Last month's miracle became this month's minimum.

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:17.440
A green number on the dashboard kept climbing.

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:20.160
So did the number of exceptions waiting for him.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:22.360
He was producing more reports than ever.

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:24.700
He was also spending more time wondering

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:26.660
whether any of them should have been reports.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:28.840
Then an unusual request arrived.

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:30.860
The figures were technically valid,

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:32.860
but the conclusion made no sense.

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:34.400
Ben stopped the workflow

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:36.440
and called the person who had asked for it.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:38.540
They needed a different question answered.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:40.900
While he was explaining that, a message

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:43.180
appeared asking why the workflow had stopped.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:45.660
Apparently, thinking was now a delay.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:47.960
Ben put his cold lunch back in the fridge.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:50.040
The dashboard measured completed items.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:52.780
It didn't measure whether an item was useful.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:55.120
It didn't count the mistake Ben prevented,

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:59.220
the confusing request he clarified, or the coworker he helped.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:02.060
Those activities took time away from the green number.

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:03.440
The number wasn't evil.

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:06.040
It was just very confident about describing

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:08.340
a small part of reality as the whole thing.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:10.580
A coworker named Jo had a suggestion.

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:12.280
Make the workflow slower.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:13.560
Add a progress bar.

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:15.920
Maybe an important looking loading message.

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:18.520
Ben could tell people the system was processing.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:20.660
Jo called this managing expectations.

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:23.880
Ben called it building a very expensive curtain.

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:25.760
Neither of them liked the idea.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:28.240
But a workplace that rewarded visible struggle

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:30.860
was making invisible efficiency feel suspicious.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:33.940
By Friday, Ben had stopped taking his walk.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:36.960
His mother received a message saying he would call later.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:38.440
Later became tomorrow.

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:41.840
Tomorrow became a weekend he spent recovering from the week.

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:44.920
The tool still saved hours on individual tasks.

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:48.240
But every saved hour immediately attracted another task.

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:49.780
He had made each trip shorter

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:51.940
while someone kept moving the finish line.

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:54.660
That evening, Mara invited him into a meeting.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:57.400
Ben expected a warning about the delayed request.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:01.040
Instead, Mara closed the door and turned her laptop around.

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:03.480
She had automated her own weekly reports.

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:06.640
Her boss had responded by giving her two more teams.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:09.160
Mara wasn't standing outside the machine.

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:11.340
She was on the next conveyor belt.

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:12.820
They stared at each other.

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:16.560
Two people who had saved time, both too busy to enjoy it.

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:19.580
Mara said she had wanted to reduce the team's backlog.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:21.900
Then the lower backlog had become evidence

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:23.380
that the team could handle more.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:25.800
Ben said he had wanted a quiet afternoon.

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:27.520
They looked at their calendars.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:30.020
The only quiet rectangle left was a lunch

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:31.700
break marked as a tentative event.

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:33.120
They could hide the tools.

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:35.800
They could pretend everything still took eight hours.

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:38.520
Or they could ask a more uncomfortable question.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:42.580
When a task gets faster, what are we actually trying to improve?

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:44.580
They wrote down three possibilities.

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:45.640
More output.

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:46.760
Better quality.

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:47.900
More time back.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:49.640
All three sounded reasonable.

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:51.980
But only one was happening automatically.

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:53.780
The other two needed an agreement.

00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:56.280
More output wasn't always a bad choice.

00:06:56.680 --> 00:06:59.160
Sometimes the team really needed to clear a backlog.

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:00.720
Time back meant deciding

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.800
that every empty space didn't need filling.

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:05.100
The problem wasn't choosing one of these.

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:08.120
The problem was pretending there had never been a choice.

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:11.600
On Monday, Ben and Mara brought a proposal to the team meeting.

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:12.760
Keep the checks.

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:14.660
Agree on a realistic workload.

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:17.620
Use some saved time to prevent recurring mistakes.

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:20.460
Protect a little of it from being instantly filled.

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:23.320
The first response was a request for more output.

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:25.700
Ben had prepared a slide about wellbeing.

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:28.820
Mara quietly moved it aside and opened the error log.

00:07:29.140 --> 00:07:31.980
They showed the green dashboard next to the work it couldn't see.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:33.780
The corrected customer records.

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:36.520
The unusual request that needed a conversation.

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:39.920
The duplicate reports that nobody actually used.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:43.380
Jo admitted receiving the same update in three different formats.

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:46.260
Someone else admitted never opening any of them.

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:47.180
For a moment,

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:50.240
the most productive thing in the room was an awkward silence.

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:52.300
They agreed to a one month trial.

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:55.120
One shared report would replace the duplicates.

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:57.480
Routine work would keep its review checks.

00:07:57.960 --> 00:07:59.840
New requests would go through a visible queue

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:01.780
instead of landing on whoever looked free.

00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:04.800
The team would track corrections as well as completions.

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:08.040
Every week, they would discuss where the saved time went.

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:10.120
It was less exciting than a revolution.

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:12.980
It was also something they could actually try.

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:15.200
The first test arrived before lunch.

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:16.920
A new urgent request appeared

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:19.420
while Ben was helping Jo understand an exception.

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:22.780
This time, Mara didn't simply add it to his pile.

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:25.300
She asked which existing task should move.

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:27.420
The person requesting it had to choose.

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:30.840
The request turned out to be less urgent than its subject line.

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:32.660
Ben returned to helping Jo.

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:34.260
The factory kept working.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:35.920
The sky remained in place.

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:38.540
Ben documented the workflow so he wasn't

00:08:38.540 --> 00:08:40.260
the only person who understood it.

00:08:40.540 --> 00:08:42.460
Jo learned to inspect the strange cases.

00:08:42.920 --> 00:08:44.980
Another coworker improved the instructions.

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:48.140
Ben stopped being the secret keeper of a magic button

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:51.160
and became part of a team that could question its own process.

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:52.940
The machine still made mistakes.

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:56.040
Now, more people knew where to look when it did.

00:08:56.360 --> 00:08:58.720
At the end of the month, they reviewed the trial.

00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:00.320
Some tasks were faster.

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:03.500
Some tasks had disappeared because nobody needed them.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:05.660
The unusual work still took time,

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:07.660
and the plan finally admitted that.

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:09.520
They hadn't solved every problem.

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:11.160
Busy weeks still happened.

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:13.860
But being efficient no longer automatically meant

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:16.160
volunteering for an unlimited amount of work.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:18.080
Ben's lunch was warm more often.

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:21.120
The tool hadn't decided how Ben's time would be used.

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:22.060
People had.

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:24.480
That didn't make changing the agreement easy.

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:26.840
It did make the agreement worth noticing.

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:28.780
A faster workflow can help.

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:32.600
It can also make an impossible workload look temporarily possible.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:35.200
If every improvement only raises the minimum,

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:37.180
the system eventually runs out

00:09:37.180 --> 00:09:39.080
of something the dashboard never measured.

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:41.100
People with enough energy to care.

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:44.080
On Friday, Ben closed his laptop at five.

00:09:44.380 --> 00:09:45.440
Then his phone lit up.

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:46.860
Could you automate our meetings?

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:48.160
He looked at Mara.

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:49.600
Mara looked at the calendar.

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:50.540
For once,

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:53.900
they scheduled a conversation before building another machine.

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:56.280
Ben went outside while it was still light.

00:09:56.620 --> 00:09:58.180
The upgrade wasn't just the button.

00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:00.260
It was the agreement around the button.

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:03.340
If you saved ten hours at work, who would get them?
